week 2 content 
This week we will explore data visualization and how to create some basic plots in R.  Work through the example files and begin the assignment. Email me if you have questions.

Objectives

Upon completion of this week, you will be able to:

Understand data visualization and its capabilities 
Be able to develop basic plots in R
Use ggplot() to create plots in R.
Readings

No reading required this week. Understand the Visualization PowerPoint. What plots are for what types of variables? Know which types of plots are applied to different variable data types. For example: scatter plots are for comparing 2 numeric variables. 



Assignments

For many assignments it is possible to use the same dataset, particularly in the case of classification . Once you find one that you are comfortable with, you can stick with that one. 

Assignment 1:  Due February 3, 6 PM . Find a dataset. Develop your own k-nn model in the same way we did with the examples using your own dataset. Begin with an idea and questions. In our example, the question would be: "What factors determine whether a tumor is malignant or benign?" Locate a dataset you want to work with and develop a k-NN model in R. Note that your target variables should be a small number of categories (like malignant and benign in the example problem). Do not use the iris dataset or any that are used in the examples (that applies to the entire course). Do not attempt to develop a model with a target variable that has several levels.  Develop a k-NN model and try several different values for k to obtain the best accuracy. Create a confusion matrix and interpret kappa, the no inform ation rate, sensitivity, and specificity. Evaluate the model and offer your conclusion. 

You can find datasets in R. Type data() and you can find them everywhere online. Kaggle has datasets.  You just type "datasets" or "classification datasets" into Googpe. 

The steps prior to running a model are fairly standard for machine learning models.  Put the data in a form conducive to analysis. Eliminate any unnecessary variables. Normalize the data (if necessary). Create training and testing data sets. 

Include str() of your data in R, a copy of your dataset file, outputs, and a complete explanation of what you did, why you did it, the results, and your conclusion.   Make sure you include comments. When in doubt give me more information than you think I need.  


